      "reflect_guidance": "The focus dimensions for this cycle should be Error consistency, Low elegance, Mid elegance, and Duplication - all have double-digit headroom and are either declining or stagnant. Avoid Abstraction fit (rework trap from SOLID false positives), Cross-module arch (diminishing returns at 91.0), and all dimensions already above 95.0. The recurring rework loops in error_consistency and mid_level_elegance suggest the subjective reviewer flags are too sensitive in these areas; any new findings should be cross-referenced against the 113 and 78 previously resolved issues respectively to avoid re-doing work. Clusters should target concrete code changes, not reviewer opinion alignment.",
      "organize_guidance": "Priority order: (1) Duplication boilerplate issues - 6/6 failing with 0.3 pass rate is the most actionable detector-level failure. (2) Error consistency - address the one open finding plus any new ones from scanning, but avoid the re-flagging trap by checking against resolved IDs. (3) Low elegance - the two open findings (lifecycle_create_inline_orchestration, normalizer_main_loop_phase_density) are concrete and actionable. (4) Mid elegance - the open pr_merge_handler_wide_boundary finding. (5) File health structural issues (6 failing). Avoid creating clusters for Abstraction fit, Convention drift, or any dimension above 93. Do not re-cluster previously resolved areas without evidence of new code changes in those files.",
      "sense_check_guidance": "Watch for three value traps: (1) Abstraction fit rework - the reviewer will keep flagging single-impl interfaces and passthrough delegation as problems; these are intentional DI patterns and fixing them would violate project architecture principles. Skip or false-positive these. (2) Test health score inflation - the 28.6-point gap between strict (87.2) and verified (58.6) means test coverage claims are not trustworthy. Any cluster claiming to fix test health should demonstrate actual coverage improvement, not just detector satisfaction. (3) Error consistency churn - with 113 resolved and 8 reopened, the dimension has the worst resolve-to-reopen ratio. New error consistency work should target the specific open finding (no_error_cause_chaining) rather than broad sweeps that get re-flagged.",
      "anti_patterns": [
        {
          "type": "rework-loop",
          "description": "Error consistency has 113 resolved issues but 8 reopens, and the dimension score DECLINED from 88.5 to 86.0 despite all that work. This is the clearest rework loop in the history.",
          "evidence": "error_consistency: 113 resolved, 8 reopened, score went from 88.5 to 86.0 (declining trend)"
        },
